   "note": "RESULTS_final.md says BLIP-VQA 'reads ~66% of faces as smiling': 66% is the corpus-wide share of smile/smiling/happy answers over all 1,012 records (people or not); within the 591 face-classified records the share is lower (see face_classified_591)."
  },
  "notes": [
   "Strict tier: whenever BLIP-VQA names an affective state (happy, sad, angry, surprised, calm, ...), the image reader's top-1 family is that state in 417 of 418 records — the NLI stage reproduces the describer's word rather than adding a reading of its own.",
   "'serious' (103 answers) is mapped a priori to neutral, but the reader returns sadness 71 / anger 15 / neutral 8 / surprise 5 / fear 4 — it reads 'serious' as negative valence. Counted as non-copy here; under a 'serious -> sadness' mapping the broad copy rate would be higher, not lower.",
   "Facial-action words ('smile' 299, 'smiling' 165) are read as joy only ~53% of the time, and only 37.7% when the description also says there are no people ('A drawing of none ...'); the remainder go to neutral or surprise. This is the leak into the scene group: 332 of the 414 people=0 scene records still say smile/smiling/happy, and the reader returns joy for 125 of them."
  ]
 },
